{"name":"Busy Bee","hex":"#f4ff00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"busy bee","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hardworking person.","Synonym of working bee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/busy_bee"}],"entries":[{"title":"Busy Bee","blocks":[{"text":"Busy Bee was an airline which operated in Norway between 1966 and 1992. Entirely based around wet lease, it conducted a mix of regional services for larger airlines and the military, as well as corporate, ad hoc and inclusive tour charters."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/63/Busy_Bee_logo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Busy_Bee","wikidata":"Q5002241"},{"title":"Busy Bee (record label)","blocks":[{"text":"Busy-Bee was a Chicago-based record label founded in 1904 that sold both cylinders and discs, often with content licensed from other labels. Their cylinders could only be played on special Busy-Bee players, which were most commonly Columbia model Q graphophones outfitted with a special, larger mandrel. This allowed the customer to only buy Busy Bee cylinders, a premium scheme similar to Aretino discs. A 1906 cylinder catalog shows artists including Gilmore's Band, Billy Murray, Arthur Collins, Bob Roberts, and Ada Jones."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Busybeepooroldman1-24-26.jpg/330px-Busybeepooroldman1-24-26.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Busy_Bee_(record_label)","wikidata":"Q28458607"},{"title":"Bee","blocks":[{"text":"Bees are winged insects that form a monophyletic clade Anthophila within the superfamily Apoidea of the order Hymenoptera, with over 20,000 known species in seven recognized families. Some species – including honey bees, bumblebees, and stingless bees – are social insects living in colonies, whereas over 90% of bee species – including mason bees, carpenter bees, leafcutter bees, and sweat bees – are solitary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg/330px-Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bee","wikidata":"Q7391"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Air_Executive_F-27_at_Basel_1978.jpg/500px-Air_Executive_F-27_at_Basel_1978.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Air_Executive_F-27_at_Basel_1978.jpg/500px-Air_Executive_F-27_at_Basel_1978.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fokker F-27 in Air Executive livery in 1978","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAir_Executive_F-27_at_Basel_1978.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Busy_Bee_F-27_at_Basel_1982.jpg/500px-Busy_Bee_F-27_at_Basel_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Busy_Bee_F-27_at_Basel_1982.jpg/500px-Busy_Bee_F-27_at_Basel_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of the three Fokker F27-100 Friendships which was taken over from Braathens SAFE, here depicted at Basel Airport in 1982","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABusy_Bee_F-27_at_Basel_1982.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Busy_Bee_737-200.jpg/500px-Busy_Bee_737-200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Busy_Bee_737-200.jpg/500px-Busy_Bee_737-200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The airline's Boeing 